# terraform/cloudflare

Zone-wide settings and the Terraform-owned DNS records for **{{ external_domain }}**.

The shape comes from `weisssrv-lib//terraform/modules/cloudflare-zone` at the
pinned ref in `main.tf`; the record inventory is `dns.tf`, and it is the only
file you normally edit.

## Running it

```bash
task terraform:init     # once per checkout, and after a module ref bump
task terraform:plan
task terraform:apply
```

The tasks inject the API token, the account ID and the GitLab state backend
through `op run`, so nothing here needs a `.tfvars` file. State lives under this
project's GitLab-managed Terraform state, name `cloudflare`.

`terraform init` fetches the library module over HTTPS. If the library
repository is private, give git a credential for it first (`git credential`
helper, a `~/.netrc` entry, or an SSH module source).

## What Terraform owns, and what it does not

| Record class | Owner | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Apex, CAA, SPF/DMARC | Terraform (`dns.tf`) | Zone-level policy; nothing in-cluster can create it |
| Service names (`app.{{ external_domain }}`) | external-dns | Created from each IngressRoute, deleted with it |
| The apex address | The DDNS CronJob | Terraform seeds it once, then ignores `content` |

Adding a service record by hand here is how the two controllers start fighting:
external-dns owns a name for as long as its IngressRoute exists.

## Destroy protection

`protected = true` on a record means the module routes it to a resource carrying
`lifecycle.prevent_destroy`: removing the map entry then fails the plan instead
of silently deleting the record. Use it for anything whose deletion is an outage
or a security regression — the apex, any name carrying SSH or a VPN endpoint,
and the whole CAA set.

Because `lifecycle` blocks cannot take variables, the flag selects a *resource
address*, so flipping it plans a destroy and create. Add a `moved {}` block for
the change, and if the record is leaving `protected`, apply its removal from the
protected set first — `prevent_destroy` blocks the destroy half.

## Credentials

| Item | Field | Used for |
|---|---|---|
| `Cloudflare Terraform Token` | `credential` | Zone:Read + DNS:Edit + Zone Settings:Edit |
| `Cloudflare Terraform Token` | `username` | The account ID |
| `Git Terraform State Token` | `credential` | The GitLab state backend |

Keep the in-cluster DNS token (cert-manager, external-dns) as a *separate* item
with DNS:Edit + Zone:Read only. Zone Settings:Edit is what lets a token turn off
strict TLS for the whole domain.

## Changing DNS provider

`dns_backend` is an answer with one implementation. Everything provider-specific
is in this directory plus two consumers: the cert-manager `ClusterIssuer`'s
DNS-01 solver and the external-dns HelmRelease. A new backend needs a module
with the same three inputs (`zone_name`, credentials, a `records` map), an
issuer solver block, and an external-dns provider block —
The template's [ARCHITECTURE.md](https://git.ericsweiss.com/eric/weisssrv-cluster-template/-/blob/main/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md) records the seam.
